# Divinity-light: Stories, Questions, and Life Together

## Scope and status

An **independent educational prototype for grades 8–12**, separate from deeper Divinity/Doctrine. Eight connected desks, 45 authored A+B combinations, 84 items, nine deterministic checkpoints. It uses the frozen reactive-chapter/hero engine at source-manifest SHA-256 `97f0c2fc2b1c422b7079980353ba6ea58c77042c2c4a568f8b136d9f8174fe83`, but has its own world ID: `divinity-light-guide-v1-20261028`. No old world or save is replaced.

This is not a complete theology course, catechism, textbook, world-religions survey, or school-approved resource. The small first guide is bounded by a single purpose: help a class prepare an exhibition that connects careful Gospel reading to historically informed, respectful, revisable action. Forty-five is the resulting content count, not a target used to pad recipes.

All contemporary students, guests, communities, observations, and follow-up reports are fictional. There are no new accounts, analytics, belief scores, personal reflection forms, or teacher dashboards. The inherited game saves ordinary game progress locally; that is not an assessment of personal faith. No real event is scheduled and no service proposal is implemented.

## Official curriculum basis, checked 28 October 2026

The following are current official webpages as retrieved, not a guarantee that every school teaches every topic in the same year or manner. Their course descriptions informed topic selection; no chunks of curriculum or textbook prose were copied.

- **[Seattle Preparatory School, Theology](https://www.seaprep.org/academics/curriculum/theology):** contextual and literary Scripture study; Gospels; Church history; conscience and decisions; world religions; Catholic social teaching; Ignatian reflection; care for creation. The guide is a selective synthesis of those topics, not Seattle Prep’s curriculum converted into a game.
- **[Georgetown Preparatory School, Academic Courses](https://www.gprep.org/academics/academic-courses):** the published sequence includes Catholic/Jesuit foundations, Bible, ethics/social justice, and world religions/Ignatian spirituality. It corroborates the breadth of a Jesuit-school guide.
- **[Jesuit High School of New Orleans, Theology](https://www.jesuitnola.org/academics/curriculum/theology-courses/):** a pre-freshman Scripture course makes an accessible grade-8 entry plausible; later courses address Church history, other religions, personal/social morality, and Catholic social teaching. This is an audience-design inference, not a school endorsement or equivalency claim.
- **[USCCB, Doctrinal Elements of a Curriculum Framework](https://www.usccb.org/resources/doctrinal-elements-curriculum-framework):** provides supporting Catholic scope and framing. The current resource page is labeled 2025; its linked PDF carries a 2011 cover and says first printed in 2008. Do not call it a newly issued 2025 curriculum. The framework includes core Christological/sacramental material and electives in Scripture, history, social life, and interreligious issues. This prototype covers only a selected portion.

