## Evidence and attribution choices

- The Gospel pairings use named passages, not generic “Religion + Kindness” cards. The only direct Gospel quotations are the brief question in Luke 10:29 and the closing instruction in Luke 10:37, attributed to the **[public-domain World English Bible Classic](https://ebible.org/eng-web/copyright.htm)**. All longer passages are original, explicitly labeled paraphrases.
- Jesus and his first followers’ Jewish setting is explicit. The Samaritan story is not an opposition between a merciful Christianity and a legalistic Judaism. No collective blame against Jews is introduced.
- **[Dei Verbum](https://www.vatican.va/archive/hist_councils/ii_vatican_council/documents/vat-ii_const_19651118_dei-verbum_en.html)** supports the Catholic attention to literary forms; the game does not turn reading a parable into denying the historical character Catholic teaching attributes to the Gospels.
- **[Trinity’s Book of Kells account](https://www.tcd.ie/about/content/bookofkells_index.php)** and **[the Library of Congress Gutenberg exhibit](https://www.loc.gov/exhibits/bibles/the-gutenberg-bible.html)** ground actual objects. The paper-copy/press operation is explicitly a classroom model. Earlier Asian printing is acknowledged, with a **[UNESCO Jikji source](https://www.unesco.org/en/articles/frank-la-rue-worlds-documentary-heritage-belongs-all)**; Gutenberg is not “the first printer.”
- Aquinas’s method is supported by **[Summa Theologiae I, q.1](https://www.newadvent.org/summa/1001.htm)**. His intellectual setting is grounded in **[Benedict XVI’s 2 June 2010 account](https://www.vatican.va/content/benedict-xvi/en/audiences/2010/documents/hf_ben-xvi_aud_20100602.html)**. The game avoids that account’s compressed generalizations about individual Arabic commentators; it claims transmission and debate, not that all Muslim philosophers held one doctrine.
- Luther’s claims come from **[the theses, especially 43–45](https://www.luther.de/en/95thesen.html)**. The **[National Library of Scotland](https://www.nls.uk/collections/stories/scottish-history/how-martin-luther-sparked-the-reformation/)** supports print circulation, not a permission-to-sin caricature. The **[current Catechism §§1471–1473](https://www.vatican.va/archive/ENG0015/__P4G.HTM)** is expressly a current explanation, not misrepresented as a 1517 reply. No contested door-nailing anecdote is taught.
- The Examen is Christian prayer, not a secular productivity checklist. The short original explanation draws on **[Loyola Press](https://www.loyolapress.com/catholic-resources/ignatian-spirituality/examen-and-ignatian-prayer/how-can-i-pray-try-the-daily-examen/)**. The learner is never required to pray, confess, or disclose beliefs.
- Other religions have their own sources: **[Chabad](https://www.chabad.org/library/article_cdo/aid/253219/jewish/A-Short-Guide-to-Shabbat-Rest.htm)** is identified as Orthodox, **[INGYouth](https://ing.org/resources/for-students/ingyouth-resources/ingyouth-answers-to-frequently-asked-questions/)** as a largely Sunni perspective that does not speak for every Muslim, and **[the Karaniya Metta Sutta](https://accesstoinsight.org/tipitaka/kn/snp/snp.1.08.amar.html)** as a Buddhist source. No copyrighted translation passage is reproduced. A modern comparison is explicitly not documented influence. The three examples are not offered as complete portraits.
- **[Nostra Aetate](https://www.vatican.va/archive/hist_councils/ii_vatican_council/documents/vat-ii_decl_19651028_nostra-aetate_en.html)** and **[Unitatis Redintegratio](https://www.vatican.va/archive/hist_councils/ii_vatican_council/documents/vat-ii_decree_19641121_unitatis-redintegratio_en.html)** are Catholic perspectives on interreligious and inter-Christian dialogue, respectively—not supposedly neutral descriptions of what others believe.
- **[USCCB social-teaching themes](https://www.usccb.org/beliefs-and-teachings/what-we-believe/catholic-social-teaching/seven-themes-of-catholic-social-teaching)** and **[Laudato Si’](https://www.vatican.va/content/francesco/en/encyclicals/documents/papa-francesco_20150524_enciclica-laudato-si.html)** support ethical framing. They do not provide imaginary empirical data about the fictional pantry or fountain.

The complete source registry lives with the Scenario. All source text is in intentional `Item.reading`; generic descriptions contain no clues. Source quotations, original summaries, present-day comparisons, and fictional cases are labeled distinctly.

## Audience and teacher use

The common route uses short, plain headings and optional readings under 90 words. Important terms are introduced rather than used as passwords: parable, Gospel, Examen, Reformation, and the distinction behind an indulgence. Successful combinations show a source-based interpretation or a defensible response to supplied case evidence; they cannot measure agreement with a doctrine.

For grades 8–9, a teacher could pair the two Gospel stories with a spoken discussion of who is heard in a case. For grades 10–12, the same artifacts can support comparison of actual source passages, omitted details, limits of an inference, and alternative defensible plans. These are suggested uses, not tested learning outcomes. No lesson duration or reading-grade score has been validated. Human classroom testing remains necessary.

Useful discussion questions stay outside the reward system: What did the comparison preserve? Which claim needs a different kind of evidence? Whose voice is still missing? What could make this proposal fail? Students may disagree with an interpretation without the game treating their beliefs as wrong.

## Deliberate limits

This first guide does not attempt systematic treatment of the Trinity, Incarnation, Paschal Mystery, all sacraments, Scripture’s entire canon, Church councils, colonialism and missions, every Reformation tradition, Hinduism, Sikhism, Indigenous traditions, or nonreligious philosophies. Some are important future modules, but adding a tokenistic card would be worse than declaring the boundary. Historical conflict is non-graphic. The scenario is not pastoral, medical, engineering, or legal advice.

## Integration contract

Only new files are supplied. `ui/campaigns/divinity-light-guide.ts` exports the complete Scenario. `research/divinity-light/integration.ts` supplies its additive catalogue helper, proposed presentation, and route. It intentionally does **not** modify the frozen six-world registry, lifecycle audit, source guards, or UI. Consequently the URL in that descriptor is **proposed, not live** until the root integrator registers it and publishes a successor. Give it a separate visible educational-guide entry; do not silently place it under deeper Divinity or claim that it replaces Doctrine.

A root integration should append the scenario once, append its lifecycle audit rows without changing prior rows, include the authored route evidence, and add this test file to its aggregate run. Then rerun content validation/build and applicable catalogue/source contracts against the actual integration. No deployment is authorized or performed by this author.

## Reproduction and verification

From this candidate root:

```sh
python experiments/tangible-campaign/research/divinity-light/author-guide.py
node experiments/tangible-campaign/research/divinity-light/generate-evidence.ts
node --test tests/divinity-light-guide.test.ts
npm run build
node --test tests/tangible-campaign.test.ts
```

The generator adds all nine checkpoints deterministically. The focused tests cover source/schema/lifecycle contracts, all canonical combinations, 40 alternate normal-highlight routes, meaningful continuity at every desk, optional readings, saved progress and Undo at every canonical step, partial goals, and already-earned entry/ending-bridge behavior. The latter uses a clearly identified suspended-entry fixture backed by actual earned tokens/history; it is not claimed as a natural fresh-player route. Existing engine/UI bytes remain unchanged. No browser/native UI testing or human enjoyment/learning claim is made.
